# Coding standards

The reviewer applies these to every C change. `scripts/check` already enforces formatting,
warnings, clang-tidy, sanitizers, the banned suppressions, dropped results, `exit` in the library
and that every line of a Unity test file runs, so they are not repeated here.

The reader is {{reader}}. Every rule serves that reader.

## Memory and ownership

- The comment of every function that allocates, frees or keeps a pointer says who owns the memory
  and who frees it.
- A buffer travels with its capacity: `char *out, size_t capacity`. Never assume a size.
- `size_t` for sizes and indexes.
- No mutable global state. Pass what a function needs.
- Recursion only with a bounded depth, stated in the comment.

## Errors

- Expected failures are return values: a status `enum` plus an out-parameter. On failure the
  out-parameter stays unchanged.
- `abort()` is for broken invariants only, never for bad input. The check cannot tell the two
  apart; the reviewer does.

## Comments

A reader can follow a file from top to bottom by its comments alone.

- Every file starts with a `///` comment that says what it is for.
- Every public function has a `///` comment in its header: what it does, its inputs, its result and
  who owns the memory.
- Explain a C construct briefly the first time a file uses it, if the reader may not know it. For
  example a compound literal or `restrict`. One short sentence.
- No filler, no praise of the code, no restating the code in words.

## Functions, modules and names

- Functions are short, do one thing and read top to bottom. Pull out a named `static` helper
  instead of nesting.
- One module is one `.h` under `include/` plus one `.c` under `src/`.
- Names say what a value is: `capacity`, `length`. Single letters only for loop indexes.
- Use the same name for the same concept everywhere: in code, tests and docs.

## Dependencies

A new library, vendored or found by CMake, needs the owner's approval.

## Tests

- New behaviour comes with a test. A bug fix comes with a test that was red before the fix.
- Every test asserts what the code must do. The check proves that each test line ran, not that
  it checks anything.
- A property test reaches the code for most of its inputs. A guard that returns early for nearly
  every input passes without testing.
- Test names describe behaviour.
- A contract that holds for every input, such as a round trip or a bound, gets a property test.
